1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,590
Grip strength is about more than just

2
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:03,070
being able to hold on.

3
00:00:03,720 --> 00:00:04,910
It’s an important indicator

4
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:06,670
of overall strength, physical function,

5
00:00:06,680 --> 00:00:08,910
and how your body performs as you age.

6
00:00:08,920 --> 00:00:11,070
So, what can a dead hang tell us?

7
00:00:11,080 --> 00:00:13,160
How long can you maintain your hold?

8
00:00:13,290 --> 00:00:16,000
Can you maintain a comfortable, controlled position?

9
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:19,350
Does one hand fatigue noticeably faster?

10
00:00:19,550 --> 00:00:22,870
And is your endurance improving with consistent training?

11
00:00:22,930 --> 00:00:24,760
These small measurements can offer insight

12
00:00:24,770 --> 00:00:28,200
into your strength, endurance, and physical capabilities over time.

13
00:00:28,210 --> 00:00:30,360
Because maintaining strength and physical function

14
00:00:30,370 --> 00:00:32,200
plays an important role in mobility,

15
00:00:32,210 --> 00:00:34,200
independence, and healthy aging.

16
00:00:34,210 --> 00:00:37,080
A dead hang is one small piece of the picture.

17
00:00:37,090 --> 00:00:38,920
Measure what matters.
